# Stage1 semantic — the `DiagnosticKind` enum (`extend Semantic: public:`): one ordinal
# per diagnostic stage1 can report, matching stage0's set.
#
# This file is over the 600-line part-file limit and cannot be split further: an enum
# declaration is a single indivisible member, and splitting the KIND itself would change
# the language surface rather than the file layout. It is kept alone so the limit is
# broken by exactly one irreducible declaration instead of by a mixed file.
#
# The wording and severity for each kind live in semantic_api_message*.elisa and
# semantic_api_severity.elisa — a new kind must be given both.

extend Semantic:
    public:
        # The category of a diagnostic. Human-readable wording and LSP severity for each
        # kind are the frontend's responsibility — see `diagnostic_message` /
        # `diagnostic_severity` in semantic_api. Consumers (LSP, CLI) render those rather
        # than re-deriving text/severity per kind. Both the parser (syntax findings) and
        # the checker (semantic findings) feed the one `SymbolTable.diagnostics` stream, so
        # this enum spans both — `Semantic::check` folds `Ast::File.errors` in via
        # `parse_error_kind`.
        const enum DiagnosticKind of u16:
            # A value-position identifier that resolves to no parameter, local, or global.
            UndefinedName
            # `==`/`!=` between raw u8& C-string pointers (reflection `.name` values):
            # compares addresses, not contents. Severity 2; name carries the operator.
            CStrPointerCompare
            # A FUNCTION whose name + signature duplicates an earlier function in the
            # same scope (an overload with a different signature is legal). Parity:
            # stage0's `duplicate declaration "f" (already defined as func)`.
            DuplicateDecl
            # A TYPE (struct/enum) whose name duplicates an earlier type in the same
            # scope. Parity: stage0's `duplicate type "P"`. Types have their own
            # namespace, so a struct and a function may share a name.
            DuplicateType
            # A mutation (`x <- v` or a compound assign) whose target is a parameter or
            # local declared without `mutable`.
            AssignImmutable
            # A parameter name repeated in the same function signature.
            DuplicateParam
            # A field name repeated in the same struct declaration. `name` is the field,
            # `expected` the struct. Parity-matched to stage0's `duplicate field`.
            DuplicateField
            # A variant name repeated in the same enum declaration. `name` is the variant,
            # `expected` the enum. Parity-matched to stage0's `duplicate variant`.
            DuplicateVariant
            # A `match` on a value of a known enum type that omits at least one variant and
            # has NO catch-all arm (`_`/binding). `name` is the enum, `expected` the first
            # missing variant. Parity-matched to stage0's `non-exhaustive match`. Sound
            # subset: fires ONLY when the scrutinee is a plain identifier whose declared
            # type is a registered enum and EVERY arm is a variant (or or-of-variants)
            # pattern — any catch-all or unclassifiable arm bails (no report).
            NonExhaustiveMatch
            # An EXPRESSION-position match/when (`x = match n:`) over an OPEN scalar domain
            # (int/char/string/float) with no catch-all `_`/binding arm — it cannot produce
            # a value for every input. `name` is the scalar family. docs/125 R2; parity with
            # stage0's `non-exhaustive integer match expression; add a final _ arm`, applied
            # uniformly to match AND when (a value-producing table must be total).
            NonExhaustiveScalarMatch
            # docs/125 §9 Tier-2: a `machine over` whose input is a closed const enum (classified
            # dispatch) has a state that either uses a `_` wildcard (rejected — spell the tags) or
            # misses a variant. `name` = state, `expected` = enum, `actual` = "_" (wildcard) or the
            # missing variant name.
            MachineTagCoverage
            # A direct call whose argument count matches no declaration of the callee
            # (fewer than the required parameters, or more than all of them).
            ArityMismatch
            # A local redefined in the same statement list: a second `x: T = v`
            # declaration, or an `x = v` binding definition when `x` is already a
            # local/param of that list. (A nested block legally SHADOWS; only
            # same-list redefinition is an error — mirrors stage0.)
            DuplicateLocal
            # A `return EXPR` in a function declared (or defaulted) `-> void`.
            ReturnValueInVoid
            # A bare `return` in a function declared to return a value.
            MissingReturnValue
            # A reference to `Enum.Variant` where `Enum` is a declared enum but
            # `Variant` is not one of its variants (expression or pattern position).
            UnknownVariant
            # An `Enum.Variant(...)` construction or destructuring pattern whose
            # value/pattern count differs from the variant's declared payload
            # fields. (A bare tag-only pattern `Enum.Variant:` is legal and exempt.)
            VariantArity
            # A literal initializer whose kind can never inhabit the declared
            # primitive type (`x: i64 = "s"`, `b: bool = 1`, ...). Only the
            # oracle-verified impossible pairs are flagged; adaptive literals
            # (int->float, int->char, char->int, float->int) are legal.
            TypeMismatch
            # An `if`/`while` condition whose inferred type family is provably
            # not bool (a numeric/string local, arithmetic result, or non-bool
            # literal). Unknown families are never flagged.
            NonBoolCondition
            # An operand of a logical `and`/`or` that the engine proves is not bool
            # (a firm int/float/string/char). `name` is the operator ("and"/"or"),
            # `actual` the operand's family. Parity with stage0's "logical operator
            # requires bool operands". Sound subset: only fires on a firmly-known
            # non-bool operand kind.
            NonBoolOperand
            # An operand of an arithmetic `+ - * / %` that the engine proves is not
            # numeric (a firm bool or string). `name` is the operator, `actual` the
            # operand's family. Parity with stage0's "operator requires numeric
            # operands". Char is numeric-adjacent (char arithmetic is legal) and never
            # flagged; unknown operands never flag.
            NonNumericOperand
            # An `==`/`!=` comparison whose two operands the engine proves live in
            # DIFFERENT comparison groups (bool / string / numeric{int,float,char}) — a
            # value that can never equal the other, e.g. `n == "s"`. `expected`/`actual`
            # are the two families. Sound subset: fires only when BOTH operands are firm
            # and cross-group; numeric-width and int/float/char mixes are compatible.
            IncompatibleComparison
            # An `is Enum.Variant` test whose left operand has a firm non-enum type.
            # `actual` is the inferred surface type of the tested value.
            InvalidVariantTestOperand
            # A known sequence scrutinee (`array`/`darray`/`view`) with an enum-variant
            # pattern at the top level, where stage0 requires a list pattern or `_`.
            InvalidTopLevelSequenceMatchArm
            # --- hints (stage1-only; stage0 is silent on these) ---
            # A parameter never referenced in the body (and not `_`-discarded).
            UnusedParam
            # A `x: T = v` local never referenced after its declaration.
            UnusedLocal
            # A `break` not lexically inside a `for`/`while` loop body. (stage1-only,
            # like the unused hints — stage0 does not diagnose it; an error since
            # it can never be valid.)
            BreakOutsideLoop
            # A `continue` not lexically inside a `for`/`while` loop body.
            ContinueOutsideLoop
            # A `for` loop whose iterable expression is a non-iterable type (a firm
            # primitive like int, float, bool, or void). Unknown families and named types
            # are never flagged (conservative, firm-typed only).
            NonIterableLoop
            # A statement that can never execute because an earlier statement in the
            # same list always exits it (`return`/`break`/`continue`). stage1-only
            # (stage0 is silent), surfaced as a Warning: the code is valid but dead.
            # Only the FIRST unreachable statement of a list is flagged. Conservative:
            # a `match`/`if` whose branches all exit is NOT treated as an exit, so code
            # after it is never falsely flagged.
            UnreachableCode
            # A `match` arm that can never match because an earlier arm already covers
            # every value it would: an exact-duplicate variant/literal, or any arm after
            # a catch-all (`_` or a bare binding). Parity-matched to stage0's
            # "unreachable because an earlier arm already matches it" ERROR. Conservative
            # — a variant arm is only treated as a dominator when its payload
            # subpatterns are all irrefutable (`_`/binding), so `E.A(1)` vs `E.A(2)` is
            # never falsely flagged.
            UnreachableMatchArm
            # A `Name{label: value, ...}` construction naming a field the struct does
            # not declare. `name` is the field, `expected` the struct. Parity-matched
            # to stage0's `struct literal "S" has no field "f"`. Sound subset: fires
            # ONLY when the constructed type is a bare identifier naming a struct with
            # a known (>=1 named field) registry entry — generic/qualified type exprs,
            # positional (unlabeled) fields, and fieldless structs all bail. (Elisa
            # structs have a closed field set — no field inheritance — so a known
            # field set is authoritative.)
            UnknownField
            # A `Name{label: a, label: b}` construction naming the same field twice.
            # `name` is the field, `expected` the struct. Parity-matched to stage0's
            # `struct literal "S" field "f" is specified more than once`. Always an
            # error regardless of field defaults/optionality, so no field-modeling is
            # needed — same registered-struct gate as UnknownField.
            DuplicateFieldInit
            # A by-label `Name{...}` construction that omits a REQUIRED field (one
            # that is neither optional `f?: T` nor defaulted `f: T = v`). `name` is
            # the missing field, `expected` the struct. Parity-matched to stage0's
            # `struct literal "S" is missing field "f"`. Sound subset: fires only in
            # PURE by-label mode (every provided field labeled) — an empty `{}` or any
            # positional/mixed field bails (positional fills fields in order).
            MissingField
            # A `<-` mutation whose target is a bare name that resolves to nothing
            # (no param/local/global). Distinct from UndefinedName: `<-` requires an
            # EXISTING target, so this points the user at `=` to introduce a local.
            # Parity: stage0's `undefined assignment target "x" (use = to introduce a
            # new local; <- requires an existing mutable target)`.
            AssignUndefinedTarget
            # An assignment whose target is not an assignable place — specifically a
            # function-call result (`g() <- v` / `g() = v`). Parity: stage0's `invalid
            # assignment target`. Only the unambiguous call-target form is flagged
            # (fields/indexes are valid places and never reported).
            InvalidAssignTarget
            # --- docs/119 value-block rules ---
            # A direct assignment to an outer binding inside a value block without
            # capture (docs/119 §4 E4). `name` is the outer binding being mutated.
            ValueBlockMutation
            # A `|capture|` header naming a binding that does not exist in outer scope
            # (docs/119 §6 E11). `name` is the undefined capture name.
            CaptureUndefined
            # A `|capture|` header naming an immutable outer binding (docs/119 §6 E10).
            # Only mutable bindings and `mutable T&` ref-parameters can be captured.
            # `name` is the immutable binding's name.
            CaptureImmutable
            # --- struct layout checks (stage1 port) ---
            # A struct field whose declared type names an unknown/unresolved type (not a
            # primitive, not a registered struct/enum). `name` is the field, `expected` the
            # struct, `actual` the unresolved type name. Conservative: refs/optionals/
            # generics (not bare names) are skipped and never flagged.
            UnknownFieldType
            # A struct that references itself directly without indirection (e.g. `struct S: x: S`
            # — infinite size). `name` is the struct, `expected` the offending field. Only the
            # direct case is flagged; mutual recursion via refs is sound and not caught here.
            RecursiveStruct
            # A `.cast[T]` between two DIFFERENT numeric primitive types (`x.cast[u32]`
            # where `x: i32`). `.cast[T]` is reinterpret-only; a numeric value conversion
            # must use a constructor `T(x)` / `x.T()`. `name` is the target type spelling.
            # Sound subset: fires only when the receiver is an identifier whose declared
            # type is a numeric primitive and the target is a different numeric primitive
            # (same-type `.cast`, ref casts, and unknown receivers stay silent).
            InvalidCast
            # --- syntax findings surfaced from the parser (Ast::ParseErrorKind) ---
            # A required token was missing.
            SyntaxExpectedToken
            # A token appeared where none was valid.
            SyntaxUnexpectedToken
            # A declaration was expected.
            SyntaxExpectedDeclaration
            # An expression was expected.
            SyntaxExpectedExpression
            # docs/125 §5 R1 — the alternatives of a `with`-arm bind different constant
            # sets, so the shared body cannot name a discriminator uniformly.
            SyntaxMismatchedWithBindings
            # Two arguments of one call name the same place (same root + overlapping
            # field path) and at least one is bound to an `lmut` parameter. A
            # linear-mutable value must be named by exactly one live binding, so an
            # `lmut` argument may not alias another argument of the same call. `name`
            # is the offending root variable. Appended last for ordinal stability.
            LmutAliasing
            # A packed-enum `if VALUE in STORE:` omitted the required explicit
            # `as Enum.Variant(...)` pattern binder.
            PackedIfPatternMissingBinder
            # An explicit `function[Type]` application targeted a uniquely known
            # function declaration with no generic parameter list.
            NonGenericSpecialization
            # `reduce_sum` received a uniquely known callback whose declared return
            # type is not a numeric accumulator type.
            ReduceSumNonNumericCallback
            # An `atomic[T]` annotation used an aggregate or other non-scalar T.
            AtomicUnsafePayload
            # A numeric atomic read-modify-write intrinsic received bool or ref T.
            AtomicRmwNonNumeric
            # A required parameter declared after an earlier parameter with a default.
            # Stage0 rejects this because callers can only omit a trailing suffix of
            # defaulted parameters. `name` is the required parameter.
            NonTrailingDefaultParam
            # A named/mixed direct function call violates parameter binding rules
            # (`unknown`, `duplicate`, `after_named`, or `missing` in `expected`).
            # `name` is the function; `actual` carries the parameter name when relevant.
            CallArgumentError
            # A named enum-constructor call violates payload binding rules. `name` is
            # the variant; `expected` is one of `mixed`, `unnamed`, `unknown`,
            # `duplicate`, or `missing`; `actual` carries the payload label when relevant.
            EnumConstructorArgumentError
            # --- stage0→stage1 diagnostic ports (batch, docs/semantic-checks) ---
            # A `<-` mutation whose target and value are the same identifier (`x <- x`) — a
            # no-op. `name` is the identifier.
            SelfAssignment
            # A comparison of a variable with itself (`x == x`, `x < x`, …) — always the
            # same result. `name` is the identifier.
            SelfComparison
            # An expression statement whose pure value is discarded and has no side effect
            # (a bare `x + 1`). No context fields.
            UnusedExpression
            # An `if`/`while` whose condition is a literal `true`/`false` (except the
            # idiomatic `while true`). `actual` carries "true"/"false".
            ConstantCondition
            # A comparison to a boolean literal (`x == true`) — reducible to the value or
            # its negation. No context fields.
            RedundantBoolCompare
            # The same module `include`d more than once in one scope. `name` is the module.
            DuplicateImport
            # A match arm whose LITERAL pattern duplicates an earlier arm's literal in the
            # same match (enum-variant duplicates use UnreachableMatchArm). `name` is the literal.
            DuplicateMatchArm
            # A division or modulo by a constant zero divisor (`x / 0`, `x % 0`). No context fields.
            DivisionByZero
            # A fixed-size array literal with the wrong element count. `name` is the array
            # variable; `expected_count` the declared size, `actual_count` the literal's count.
            ArrayLiteralArity
            # A parameter/return/local/const type annotation naming an unresolved type.
            # `name` is the type. Conservative: only lowercase, non-primitive, non-registered
            # bare names (shares is_unresolved_type with UnknownFieldType); uppercase/generic/
            # qualified names are never flagged.
            UnknownTypeName
            # A dict literal with two entries whose LITERAL keys are equal (`{1: a, 1: b}`).
            # `name` is the key text for string/char keys, "" for int keys.
            DuplicateDictKey
            # A set literal containing the same LITERAL element twice (`{1, 2, 1}`).
            # `name` is the element text for string/char, "" for int.
            DuplicateSetElement
            # A constant integer index into a fixed-size array local that is provably out of
            # range (`xs: i64[3]` then `xs[5]`). `name` is the array; `expected_count` its
            # size, `actual_count` the offending index (clamped to 0 when negative).
            IndexOutOfBounds
            # An arithmetic op of an identifier with ITSELF whose result is a constant:
            # `x - x` (0), `x / x` (1), `x % x` (0). `name` is the identifier.
            SelfArithmetic
            # A boolean double negation (`not not x`) — just the value itself.
            DoubleNegation
            # A `while true:` loop whose body contains no break or return at any depth.
            InfiniteLoopNoExit
            # An arithmetic identity with a literal RIGHT operand that makes the operation
            # a no-op or a constant: `x*1`, `x*0`, `x/1`, `x%1`, `x+0`, `x-0`.
            RedundantArithmetic
            # A `for` loop over a literal integer range that is provably empty
            # (`for i in 5..<5:`); the body never runs. `name` is the loop variable.
            EmptyRange
            # A `<-` mutation of a for-loop iteration variable inside the loop body; the
            # variable is rebound each iteration, so the write is dead. `name` is the variable.
            AssignToLoopVar
            # A comparison whose operands are BOTH integer literals (`1 == 2`), a
            # compile-time constant. `actual` carries the computed "true"/"false".
            ConstantComparison
            # An `elif` whose condition structurally repeats an earlier condition in the
            # same if/elif chain — the later branch can never run. Conservative: only
            # Ident/literal/Field/Binary/Unary/Paren shapes compare; anything else never flags.
            DuplicateCondition
            # A bare expression statement calling a known non-void function, discarding
            # its result. `name` is the function, `expected` its return type. Unique
            # (non-overloaded) bare-ident callees only; submit-wrapped calls exempt.
            DiscardedCallResult
            # A call to a known `-> void` function used as a VALUE (`x = g()`, VarDecl
            # initializer, or returned). `name` is the function. `<-` re-threading of
            # lmut helpers is the canonical idiom and is never flagged.
            VoidValueUse
            # A LITERAL argument at a direct call that can never inhabit the parameter's
            # declared type (`g("s")` into i64) — the locals impossible-pair oracle
            # applied at call sites. `name` the fn, `expected` the param type, `actual`
            # the literal kind. Adaptive pairs (int→char/float) are legal and silent.
            LiteralArgTypeMismatch
            # A shift by a constant count outside 0..63 — wrong for every Elisa integer
            # width. `expected_count`=64, `actual_count`=the count (0 when negative).
            OversizedShift
            # A shift by a negative constant count — never valid for any Elisa integer width.
            NegativeShift
            # A `<-` mutation assignment whose target is declared with a primitive type range
            # and whose integer literal value cannot fit that range (`x: mutable u8 = 0; x <- 300`).
            # Only the six types u8/u16/u32/i8/i16/i32 are checked; unknown types never flag.
            LiteralAssignOutOfRange
            # A comparison between an identifier of a fixed-width integer type
            # {u8,u16,u32,i8,i16,i32} and a literal value that falls outside that type's
            # representable range — the comparison result is vacuous (always true/false).
            LiteralComparisonImpossible
            # An enum variant payload field name that appears more than once in the
            # same variant's field list (duplicate positional-field indicators are not
            # flagged). `name` is the field, `expected` is the variant.
            DuplicateVariantField
            # Both operands of a logical `and` or `or` are structurally identical and
            # side-effect-free. `name` is "and" or "or".
            IdenticalLogicalOperands
            # An if (statement or value form) whose then and else branches contain
            # structurally identical statements/expressions, making one branch dead.
            IdenticalBranches
            # A `for` loop over a literally-empty collection (empty array literal `[]`,
            # empty set literal `{}`, or empty dict literal `{}`); the body never runs.
            # `name` is the loop variable.
            EmptyIterable
            # An `import` statement whose module name is never referenced anywhere in the
            # file — a dead import. Module is considered used if qualified as `Mod::name`,
            # accessed via `Mod.field`, or referenced as a plain identifier `Mod`.
            UnusedImport
            # A == or != comparison where at least one operand is a floating-point value
            # (float literal or variable typed f32/f64). Floating-point equality is unreliable
            # due to precision issues; use a tolerance-based comparison instead.
            FloatEquality
            # A negative constant integer index on a sequence type (darray, fixed array,
            # view/sview). Dict-typed or unknown receivers never flag.
            NegativeIndex
            # A pattern or loop variable binds the same name twice within a single arm or loop.
            # `name` is the duplicate binding name.
            DuplicatePatternBinding
            # A value assigned to a variable is immediately overwritten in the next
            # statement without the initial value being read. `name` is the variable.
            # Only flags plain `<-` assignment, not compound operators.
            ImmediateOverwrite
            # A `%` (modulo/remainder) with a literal zero right operand — always a
            # runtime trap. Reported distinctly from DivisionByZero (which owns `/`).
            ModuloByZero
            # A bit-shift (`<<`/`>>`) with a literal zero count — a redundant no-op.
            # Disjoint from OversizedShift (count>=64) and NegativeShift (count<0).
            ShiftByZero
            # A logical `and`/`or` where one operand is a boolean literal (`x and true`,
            # `x or false`, ...) — the operator is redundant or the result is constant.
            LogicalConstantOperand
            # The same decorator name applied more than once to one declaration.
            DuplicateDecorator
            # A `continue` that is the last statement of a loop body — a no-op. Only the
            # loop body's own final statement is flagged, never a nested-block continue.
            RedundantContinue
            # A logical-not applied directly to an `==`/`!=` comparison (`not (a == b)`);
            # the opposite comparison operator is clearer.
            NegatedComparison
            # --- batch 7: type-flow tier (ride the infer_expression_type engine) ---
            # A bare `object.field` where `object` infers to a firm scalar primitive
            # (int/float/bool/char) — a primitive has no fields. `actual` is the family.
            # Method calls (`x.f()`) and `.cast[T]` type-applications are excluded.
            FieldAccessOnPrimitive
            # An `object[i]` index where `object` infers to a firm scalar primitive
            # (int/float/bool/char) — not indexable. `actual` is the family. Strings,
            # named types (darray/dict/view/custom), and `.cast[T]` are excluded.
            IndexNonIndexable
            # A FIRM (non-literal) positional argument at a direct call to a uniquely-named
            # function whose inferred family can never inhabit the parameter's declared type
            # (`g(s)` with s: cstr into `def g(k: i64)`). `name` the fn, `expected` the param
            # type, `actual` the arg family. Literal args are owned by LiteralArgTypeMismatch.
            FirmArgTypeMismatch
            # A REFERENCE binding (lmut / mutable T&) passed to a BY-VALUE parameter of the
            # same struct: a borrow cannot inhabit a value slot. `name` the fn, `expected` the
            # value param type, `actual` the base struct name (rendered as `mutable <actual>&`).
            RefArgValueParam
            # A `@noalloc` function that uses the `Memory.Allocate` effect — directly (a
            # `can Memory.Allocate:` block in its body) or transitively (it calls a function that
            # does). `name` is the function. stage0 rejects it.
            NoallocAllocates
            # A loop `invariant P` whose single-comparison predicate provably does NOT hold at the
            # loop variable's constant entry value (`i: usize = 0` then `invariant i >= 5`) — stage0
            # cannot establish it on entry. Sound decidable subset: only single comparisons over a
            # constant-entry loop variable; free variables / non-comparisons decline.
            LoopInvariantNotEstablished
            # A call whose argument for a `requires`-constrained parameter is an unconstrained
            # refinement-free variable of the caller — stage0 cannot prove the precondition and
            # warns "precondition of X could not be proven statically at this call". `name` = the
            # callee. Same maximally-conservative fact model as RefinementArgNotProven.
            PreconditionUnproven
            # A growable container parameter (`darray`/`dict`/`set`/`dstr`) passed BY VALUE is
            # GROWN in the body, but a by-value header copy makes that growth invisible to the
            # caller. `name` is the container's surface type, `expected` the parameter name.
            # Suppressed when the parameter is returned bare (build-and-return) or is a `&`.
            ByValueGrownContainer
            # A `match` arm guard whose inferred family is firmly non-bool (`_ if n:` with
            # n: i64). `actual` is the family. Mirrors NonBoolCondition for arm guards;
            # only fires on a firm family (never Unknown).
            NonBoolMatchGuard
            # A `.cast[T]` whose receiver already has declared type T — a no-op reinterpret.
            # `name` is the type. Disjoint from InvalidCast (which owns DIFFERENT-numeric casts).
            RedundantCast
            # A bare `object.field` where `object` infers to a registered struct with a known
            # closed field set that has no such field. `name` the field, `expected` the struct.
            # Sound: any program-wide function of that name (property/UFCS) or method call bails.
            UnknownFieldAccess
            # --- batch 8: type-flow tier round 2 ---
            # A `Type{label: value}` struct-literal field whose value's type can never inhabit the
            # field's declared bare-primitive type. `name` the field, `expected` the field type,
            # `actual` the literal-kind/family. Only labeled fields on registered structs.
            ConstructFieldTypeMismatch
            # A FIRM PRIMITIVE value bound where the declared type is a registered STRUCT (`q: P = 5`,
            # `return 3` in `-> P`). `name` is the var name or "return", `expected` the struct,
            # `actual` the value family. Enums excluded; Construct/call/Named values (Unknown) never fire.
            NamedTypeMismatch
            # A VOID-typed argument at a call (`g(h())` with h: -> void). `name` the callee (or "").
            # Disjoint from FirmArgTypeMismatch (family walls, never Void) and VoidValueUse (x=/return).
            VoidArgument
            # A firm non-numeric (bool/string) operand of a shift/bitwise binary `<< >> & | ^`. Static
            # message. Disjoint from NonNumericOperand which owns `+ - * / %`; Named operands never fire.
            NonNumericShiftOperand
            # A compound assignment `x OP= v` (`+= -= *= /= %= <<= >>= &= |= ^=`) with a firm non-numeric
            # (bool/string) operand. Static message. Named LHS/RHS never fire (operator overloading).
            CompoundAssignNonNumeric
            # Indexing a STRING receiver, or a POSITIONAL container (array/darray), with a firm
            # non-numeric (bool/string/tuple) index (`s[name]`, `xs[name]`). `actual` is the index type
            # spelled as stage0 spells it (see stage0_index_spelling), NOT the coarse family. Disjoint
            # from IndexNonIndexable (scalar receiver); dict/set/Named receivers skip — a dict is indexed
            # BY ITS KEY TYPE, which DictIndexKeyTypeMismatch owns.
            StringIndexNonNumeric
            # --- batch 9: type-flow tier round 3 (void family + tail) ---
            # A bare `object.field` where `object` infers to Void. Void sibling of FieldAccessOnPrimitive.
            VoidFieldAccess
            # An `object[i]` index where `object` infers to Void. Void sibling of IndexNonIndexable.
            VoidIndex
            # A call whose callee is an identifier of firm scalar-primitive type (`n()` where n: i64).
            # `name` the callee, `actual` its family. Fn-typed locals infer Unknown and never fire.
            CallNonFunction
            # A UFCS method call `recv.m()` where two or more free functions named `m` accept the
            # receiver's type with an IDENTICAL first-parameter signature — genuinely ambiguous.
            # `name` the method, `expected` the receiver type.
            UfcsAmbiguousCall
            # A shift `<< >>` with a firm FLOAT operand (numeric but not integral). Disjoint from
            # NonNumericShiftOperand (bool/string operand).
            ShiftOperandNonIntegral
            # A void operand of an arithmetic/logical/comparison binary or unary-`not` operator. Owns only
            # operator positions (field/index/condition/arg are other checks).
            VoidOperand
            # An `if`/`while` condition that infers Void. Void sibling of NonBoolCondition (firm non-bool).
            VoidCondition
            # A ternary `A if C else B` whose two branches are firm and in different comparison-groups
            # (`5 if c else "no"`). `expected`/`actual` the two families. Numeric mixes are compatible.
            TernaryBranchMismatch
            # A fixed-array literal (`xs: i64[2] = [1, "s"]`) element whose type can't inhabit the annotated
            # element type. `expected` the element type, `actual` the literal-kind/family.
            ArrayLiteralElementTypeMismatch
            # Indexing a STRING receiver with a firm FLOAT index (`s[1.5]`) — numeric but not integral.
            # `actual` the index family. Disjoint from StringIndexNonNumeric (bool/string index).
            StringIndexNonIntegral
            # --- batch 10: type-flow tier round 4 (void tail + collection-literal element typing) ---
            # A void value used as an element of an array/set literal or a key/value of a dict literal.
            VoidCollectionElement
            # A void operand of a unary `-`/`~`. Sibling of VoidOperand (which owns `not` + binaries).
            VoidUnaryOperand
            # A `match`/`catch` scrutinee that infers Void. (catch-desugared error-set matches are excluded.)
            VoidMatchScrutinee
            # A firm non-numeric (bool/string) operand of an ordering comparison `< > <= >=`. Disjoint
            # from IncompatibleComparison (`== !=`). Named operands never fire (overloadable).
            OrderingNonNumeric
            # A `for` range `A..<B` whose bound infers to a firm Float/String or Void (non-integral).
            # `actual` the offending family.
            RangeBoundNonIntegral
            # A `darray[T]` literal element whose type can't inhabit T. `expected` T, `actual` the family.
            # Disjoint from ArrayLiteralElementTypeMismatch (fixed array T[N]).
            DArrayElementTypeMismatch
            # A `set[T]` literal element whose type can't inhabit T. `expected` T, `actual` the family.
            SetElementTypeMismatch
            # A `dict[K,V]` literal VALUE whose type can't inhabit V. `expected` V, `actual` the family.
            DictValueTypeMismatch
            # A `dict[K,V]` literal KEY whose type can't inhabit K. `expected` K, `actual` the family.
            DictKeyTypeMismatch
            # A call whose callee is an identifier of Named (struct/enum) type (`p()`). `name` the callee,
            # `actual` the type. Sound: Elisa has no callable-struct/__call__ mechanism. Sibling of CallNonFunction.
            CallNamedNonFunction
            # --- batch 11: declared-shape agreement ---
            # A `match`/`Expr.Match` arm whose `Pattern.Struct` names a DIFFERENT registered struct than
            # the scrutinee's inferred struct type. `name` the scrutinee struct, `expected` the pattern struct.
            StructPatternTypeMismatch
            # A `match` arm whose variant pattern's enum qualifier is a DIFFERENT registered enum than the
            # scrutinee's enum. `name` the scrutinee enum, `expected` the arm's enum.
            MatchArmEnumMismatch
            # A `.cast[T]` that crosses the bool boundary (bool↔numeric/string/char) — never a valid
            # reinterpret. `expected` the source type, `actual` the target. Disjoint from InvalidCast
            # (numeric↔numeric value-conversion) and the allowed string↔numeric pointer cast.
            InvalidBoolCast
            # An assignment (`<-` or a compound op) to a struct field that was not declared
            # `mutable`. `name` the field. Sound: fires only when the object is a registered
            # struct AND the field is registered AND declared non-mutable.
            FieldImmutableAssign
            # First effect-system diagnostic: a `panic(...)` performed with no enclosing
            # `can`/`trusted` grant block and no signature effect grant. Warning. Sound
            # subset of stage0's effect-authority warnings — over-grants (any grant block
            # suppresses) so it never fires where stage0 is silent. See check_ungranted_panic.
            UngrantedPanic
            # A call to a uniquely-resolved function that declares signature effects
            # (`can[...]`), made at a site with no enclosing grant block. `name` the callee.
            # Warning. Same over-granting sound-subset stance as UngrantedPanic.
            UngrantedEffectCall
            # A machine `decreases` measure whose type is not an integer (`decreases flag`
            # over a bool param). Error. `actual` carries the offending type name.
            MachineDecreasesType
            # A `mutable T[State]&` typestate parameter passed to a call whose matching param
            # is a mutable ref (so it may widen the state), with no `ensures p => <state>`
            # declaring the transition. Error. `name` the param, `expected` the function.
            TypestateWidenedByCall
            # A `mutable T[State]&` typestate parameter whose derived state is changed by a
            # top-level field write to a DIFFERENT state and not handed back (no
            # `ensures p => <state>`). Error. `name` param, `expected` fn, `actual` new state.
            TypestateNotRestored
            # A `static def` invoked at compile time (via a `static assert`) whose body
            # unconditionally calls `error(MSG)`. Error. `name` carries MSG.
            StaticError
            # A compile-time `static def` evaluation that made more than 64 nested calls
            # (runaway/too-deep recursion). Error.
            StaticEvalDepthExceeded
            # A function's `ensures p => State` postcondition is violated: the derived-state
            # flow leaves p in a DIFFERENT state at return. Error. `name` param, `expected`
            # the fn, `actual` the tracked state.
            TypestateEnsuresUnproven
            # A `requires`/`ensure` contract statement that is not among the leading
            # statements of its function body (an ordinary statement precedes it). Error.
            # Purely positional; `decreases`/`invariant`/`changes` are exempt.
            ContractNotFirst
            # An `ensure` postcondition names `result` in a `-> void` function, where `result`
            # is unbound (no return value, no param/symbol named `result`). Error.
            ContractEnsureResultVoid
            # A `dict[K, V]` whose key type K is an affine ("linear handle") struct. Error.
            # `actual` is the offending type name.
            DictKeyAffine
            # A `set[T]` whose element type T is an affine ("linear handle") struct. Error.
            # `actual` is the offending type name.
            SetElementAffine
            # Address-of was applied to a user-declared affine/linear struct value.
            AffineAddress
            # A mutable global stores a user-declared affine/linear struct value.
            AffineGlobal
            # A function parameter is a reference to an affine or affine-containing value.
            AffineReference
            AffineUseAfterConsume
            AffineMoveRequired
            AffineScopeExit
            # A `decreases` termination clause on a function that makes NO calls at all (so it
            # cannot be recursive) — the clause is unused. Warning. `name` is the function.
            UnusedDecreases
            # A `ghost` struct field with a default value. Ghost fields are verification-only
            # and erased, so a runtime initializer is meaningless. Error. `name` is the field.
            GhostFieldDefault
            GhostLocalRead
            # A `const enum X of T` whose backing type T is a known non-integer scalar. Error.
            # `name` the enum, `expected` the offending storage type name.
            ConstEnumStorageNotInt
            # A `const enum X of T` member whose literal value does not fit storage type T. Error.
            # `name` the enum, `expected` the member, `actual` the storage type.
            ConstEnumValueOutOfRange
            # A statement-position `raise` inside a function whose signature declares no
            # `error[...]` return set (docs/62 §3.3). Error. Sound: the parser records every
            # error-set function's line, so an error-set function is never flagged; a `raise`
            # buried in a sub-expression is not at statement position and is a sound miss.
            RaiseWithoutErrorSet
            # A `@region` provenance qualifier on a struct field of scalar type (`x: i32 @r`).
            # Scalars carry no region, so the annotation is meaningless. Error. `name` is the
            # field, `actual` the region name, `expected` the scalar type name.
            RegionAnnotationOnScalarField
            # A packed bit group (`flags: bitset:` / `layout: bitfield:`) with two members of
            # the same name. Error. `name` the duplicated member, `expected` the group field.
            DuplicateBitGroupMember
            # A struct that declares named typestates in its header (`struct X[state A | B]:`)
            # but carries no `derive state:` block in its body — the states can never be
            # assigned, so the declaration is ill-formed. Error. `name` is the struct.
            NamedStatesWithoutDerive
            # An explicitly state-qualified struct construction whose known literal
            # fields make that state's derived predicate false. `name` is the struct and
            # `expected` the requested state.
            DerivedStateConstructorMismatch
            # A call argument whose derived state changed after a predicate-dependent
            # field write. `detail` is the struct, `expected`/`actual` are states.
            DerivedStateArgumentMismatch
            UngrantedPointerCast
            DenseNodeKeyRootMismatch
            ThreadTransferStoreDependency
            ThreadTransferRegionDependency
            # A `mutable bool` local whose writes all live inside >= 2 distinct branch
            # statements at its declaring level, read after the last branch-writing
            # statement (docs/125 flow-flag lint; no stage0 counterpart). The boolean is
            # smearing state-machine state into a variable. Warning. `name` is the flag,
            # `expected_count` the number of branch statements that write it.
            FlowFlagStateMachine
            # An `if` used as a VALUE (a bare `a if c` ternary with no `else`, or a
            # docs/119 block-value tail `if` with no `else`) — a value-producing branch
            # that yields nothing on its false path. docs/125 branch totality: the
            # ternary-requires-else invariant. Parity: stage0 rejects these as the parse
            # error "expected else" (ternary) and semantic "docs/119 E7" (block-value);
            # stage1 unifies both under this one check. Error. The surviving
            # `Expr.If(_, _, Expr.Invalid)` node is the sole signature (legal guards are
            # desugared to `Stmt.If` in the parser, so none reach here).
            IfValueMissingElse
            # A `match` arm's tuple pattern has a different number of sub-patterns than the
            # tuple scrutinee has positions — e.g. `match a, b:` with an arm `(x, y, z):`.
            # Fires ONLY when the scrutinee is a tuple literal (arity known from the AST) and
            # the arm pattern is a tuple pattern of a different arity; every other shape bails,
            # so there are no false positives. Error.
            TuplePatternArityMismatch
            # An or-pattern's alternatives bind different sets of names — e.g.
            # `A(x) | B(y):`. Every alternative of an `A | B | …` pattern must introduce the
            # same binding names, since the arm body may run under any alternative. Purely
            # structural (binding names collected from the AST); fires only when two
            # alternatives' name SETS differ, so no false positives. Error.
            OrPatternBindingMismatch
            # A membership test `x in RHS` whose right-hand side is a CONTAINER-typed
            # value — stage0 requires a list literal or tokenset on the right of `in`
            # (parity: "membership operator requires a list literal or tokenset on the
            # right-hand side, got darray[...]"). Fires only on a firm structural
            # Container (annotated local / registered field / unique call). Error.
            MembershipRhsContainer
            # A `xs.push(v)` on a darray-typed value whose single argument can never
            # inhabit the darray's element type (`xs: darray[i64]; xs.push("s")`).
            # `expected` the element type, `actual` the argument's literal kind or firm
            # family. Parity: stage0's "darray push expects i64, got …". Error.
            DarrayPushTypeMismatch
            # A `d[k] <- v` / `d[k]` dict access whose KEY can never inhabit the
            # dict's declared key type (`d: dict[i64, V]; d["x"] <- v`). `expected`
            # the key type, `actual` the key's literal kind or firm family. Parity:
            # stage0's "dict index expects key of type i64, got …". Error.
            DictIndexKeyTypeMismatch
            # A value-constructor call `T(x)` whose argument can never convert:
            # a container into a scalar (`i64(xs)`) or a numeric into bool
            # (`bool(3)`). `expected` the target type, `actual` the source.
            # Parity: stage0's "invalid cast from int to bool". Error.
            InvalidCtorCast
            # A MODULE name used as a value (`x = M`, `M.member` — `.` accesses
            # value members). `name` is the module. Parity: stage0's
            # "M" is a namespace; write M::member. Error. Fires only when every
            # symbol of that name is a Module and no local/param shadows it.
            NamespaceUsedAsValue
            # A packed-enum move destructure explicitly names a Store belonging to a
            # different packed enum. `expected` is the moved enum owner and `actual`
            # the store's enum owner.
            PackedMoveStoreMismatch
            # A record update `value{field = replacement}` names a field absent from the
            # firmly-known registered struct type of `value`. `name` is the field and
            # `expected` is the struct name. Error.
            RecordUpdateUnknownField
            # `%` with a firmly-known float operand. Elisa modulo is integral-only.
            ModuloOperandNonIntegral
            # A POSITIONAL container — array or darray — indexed by a firmly-known float
            # value. `actual` is the offending index type, spelled as stage0 spells it
            # ("f64", not the "float" family).
            ArrayIndexNonIntegral
            # A for-loop range whose explicit literal stride is zero.
            RangeStepZero
            # A consuming `move value as Struct(...)` positional destructure has a
            # different binding count from the registered struct field count.
            MoveStructPatternArityMismatch
            # An anonymous `let {field}` destructure selects a field absent from the
            # firmly-known struct type of its right-hand side.
            LetDestructureUnknownField
